1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,360
In games, jumping is one of the most familiar actions,
performed with a single button.

2
00:00:05,580 --> 00:00:09,740
But jumping is more than just a way
to reach higher places.

3
00:00:09,960 --> 00:00:18,040
It determines how far players can go, when they must take risks,
and how much freedom they have in the air.

4
00:00:18,260 --> 00:00:25,860
Even with the same obstacle, changing the jump height, speed,
or air control can completely transform how a game feels.

5
00:00:26,080 --> 00:00:34,160
So while jumping may look simple, it hides countless design choices
that can change the entire feel of a game.

6
00:00:34,380 --> 00:00:41,260
Let's explore the basic physics of jumping
and why every game designs it differently.

7
00:00:41,910 --> 00:00:48,230
In many games, a constant gravitational force is applied
every frame while the character is airborne.

8
00:00:48,450 --> 00:00:51,490
Let's say the initial upward velocity is five.

9
00:00:51,710 --> 00:00:56,430
Then, each frame, we add one unit
of downward acceleration.

10
00:00:56,650 --> 00:01:02,570
A character rising at a velocity of five
slows to four, then three, then two.

11
00:01:03,220 --> 00:01:08,180
When the velocity reaches zero,
the character reaches the peak of the jump.

12
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:11,040
But gravity does not stop.

13
00:01:11,260 --> 00:01:16,540
The character now falls faster and faster,
with downward velocities of one, two, three, and four.

14
00:01:16,760 --> 00:01:23,880
On level ground, the character usually lands when the falling speed
is close to the initial upward speed.

15
00:01:24,530 --> 00:01:30,770
So far, we have been discussing movement on the Y-axis,
or vertical movement.

16
00:01:30,990 --> 00:01:37,070
Horizontal movement on the X-axis often maintains its speed
or slows down only slightly.

17
00:01:37,290 --> 00:01:45,290
Actual games also use top speed and momentum, but the key point is
that every jump should serve the purpose of its game.

18
00:01:45,940 --> 00:01:50,500
In Super Mario Bros.,
the upward movement is relatively slow.

19
00:01:50,720 --> 00:01:54,560
This means gravity is fairly weak
while Mario is rising.

20
00:01:54,780 --> 00:02:01,580
After the peak, however, the velocity changes quickly,
making the descent much faster than the ascent.

21
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:07,400
This also works well with a system where jump height changes
depending on how long the button is held.

22
00:02:08,050 --> 00:02:12,450
The upward movement in Metroid
is also quite slow.

23
00:02:12,670 --> 00:02:18,350
The falling speed is slow as well,
giving the jump a light, floating quality.

24
00:02:18,570 --> 00:02:26,330
Since firing at the right height matters in this game,
the design may help players adjust that height more easily.

25
00:02:26,980 --> 00:02:33,460
Ghosts 'n Goblins, by contrast, uses a fairly low jump
with almost no horizontal control in the air.

26
00:02:33,680 --> 00:02:37,520
In other words, the landing point is determined
the moment the jump begins.

27
00:02:37,740 --> 00:02:44,540
That lack of freedom can be limiting,
but it also makes every jump a careful commitment.

28
00:02:44,760 --> 00:02:50,200
That same restriction also enables
hit-and-run attacks while retreating.

29
00:02:50,850 --> 00:02:55,170
Now consider Zangief's Spinning Piledriver
in Street Fighter II.

30
00:02:55,390 --> 00:03:01,790
From Street Fighter II Turbo onward,
its falling speed is faster than its rising speed.

31
00:03:02,010 --> 00:03:06,810
This makes it feel as though the opponent
is being slammed into the ground with greater force.

32
00:03:07,030 --> 00:03:13,590
Even with the same trajectory, changing only the speed
can dramatically alter impact and game feel.

33
00:03:14,240 --> 00:03:20,560
In Super Smash Bros., every jump begins with a very short
preparatory animation called a jump squat.

34
00:03:20,780 --> 00:03:28,540
Older games gave each fighter a different jump-squat duration,
but today those timings are short and mostly standardized.

35
00:03:28,760 --> 00:03:35,640
Removing this preparation entirely could make players jump
by accident when attempting an up smash.

36
00:03:35,860 --> 00:03:39,460
That is why it cannot simply be reduced to zero frames.

37
00:03:40,110 --> 00:03:47,230
With every Smash Bros. game, the developers reconsider
whether jumping really needs to work the way it does.

38
00:03:47,450 --> 00:03:55,050
Super Smash Bros. Ultimate revived a technique
previously used in Kirby Super Star.

39
00:03:55,270 --> 00:04:01,910
The jump starts with a powerful upward velocity,
followed by extremely strong gravity for a few frames.

40
00:04:02,130 --> 00:04:08,290
The character shoots upward, decelerates sharply,
and then transitions to normal gravity.

41
00:04:08,510 --> 00:04:14,030
This helps slightly delayed player reactions
still feel natural and responsive.

42
00:04:14,680 --> 00:04:20,840
Now let's look at some more recent games
that approach jumping in completely different ways.

43
00:04:21,060 --> 00:04:28,020
In Jump King, holding the jump button charges power,
and releasing it launches the character.

44
00:04:28,240 --> 00:04:32,800
Once launched, neither the direction nor the power
can be adjusted in midair.

45
00:04:33,020 --> 00:04:41,340
Accurately predicting how long to hold the button
becomes the central source of tension in the game.

46
00:04:41,990 --> 00:04:46,790
Celeste creates precise control
using the opposite approach.

47
00:04:47,010 --> 00:04:55,970
Hold the jump button to reach full height, or release it early
to trigger stronger gravity and cut the ascent short.

48
00:04:56,190 --> 00:05:03,310
Because the initial velocity is always the same,
hold duration alone gives fine control over jump height.

49
00:05:03,530 --> 00:05:09,450
For a game built around precise platforming,
this immediate height control is essential.

50
00:05:10,100 --> 00:05:16,420
Mega Man X's dash jump shows what happens
when vertical and horizontal movement are separated.

51
00:05:16,640 --> 00:05:23,680
Whether jumping from a walk or a dash,
the maximum height and airtime remain the same.

52
00:05:23,900 --> 00:05:29,980
Gravity affects only vertical velocity,
while horizontal velocity is preserved.

53
00:05:30,200 --> 00:05:36,520
The dash jump therefore increases only travel distance,
serving as a pure reward for efficient movement.

54
00:05:37,170 --> 00:05:42,130
So, is there one correct way
to make jumping fun? Not necessarily.

55
00:05:42,350 --> 00:05:49,150
What matters more than height or speed is the kind of decisions
and emotions the jump creates for the player.

56
00:05:49,370 --> 00:05:57,850
Should players measure the distance before jumping, steer in midair,
or feel a powerful impact when they land?

57
00:05:58,070 --> 00:06:06,310
Adjust the initial velocity, gravity, air control, and startup frames,
then playtest until you find the feeling you want.

58
00:06:06,530 --> 00:06:13,330
These hidden touches make movement feel clear and responsive,
bringing depth and fun to even a single jump.
